,title,link,text,language,family
0,Arawakan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arawakan_languages,"Arawakan, also known as Maipurean (also Maipuran), is a language family spoken amongst various Indigenous peoples in South America. Branches migrated to Central America and the Greater Antilles and Lesser Antilles in the Caribbean and the Atlantic, including what is now the Bahamas.",Yawalapití,Maipurean
1,Yawalapiti - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yawalapiti,"May 25, 2026 - The Yawalapiti (also Jaulapiti, Yaulapiti, or Yawalapití) are an Indigenous tribe in the Amazonian Basin of Brazil. The name is also spelled Iaualapiti in Portuguese. The current village Yawalapiti is situated more to the south, between the Tuatuari and Kuluene River.",Yawalapití,Maipurean
2,Yawalapití language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yawalapití_language,"April 9, 2026 - Nominal and verbal prefixes intervene in the formation of possessive phrases, and can indicate the subject or the object of a verb phrase. The set of possessive prefixes of Yawalapiti is very similar to those of the other languages of the Arawakan branch.",Yawalapití,Maipurean
3,Maipure language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maipure_language,"May 28, 2026 - Maipure (Maypure, Mejepure, Maipure: maipùri jucuàre [maiˈpuɺi jukuˈaɺɛ]) is an extinct Arawakan (Maipurean) language formerly spoken in the Venezuelan state of Amazonas. The language began declining, and eventually went extinct, around the late 18th century.",Yawalapití,Maipurean
4,Yawalapiti Dance - song and lyrics by Traditional Music ...Yawalapiti - song and lyrics by A.F.C.R Tribo Muirapinima ...POVO YAWALAPITI | Videos & Movies on VimeoArawakan languages - Wikipedia,https://open.spotify.com/track/3O1cHznC339xHzESa2FyCp,"Listen to Yawalapiti Dance on Spotify. Traditional Music, Alexandre Elias · Song · 2017. Listen to Yawalapiti on Spotify. A.F.C.R Tribo Muirapinima · Song · 2014. POVO YAWALAPITI a film by Vincent Moon & Priscilla Telmon, Petites Planètes produced by Fernanda Abreu, Feever Filmes The Yawalapiti from Xingu are famed for their music centered around the sacred flutes. Arawakan, also known as Maipurean (also Maipuran), is a language family spoken amongst various Indigenous peoples in South America. Branches migrated to Central America and the Greater Antilles and Lesser Antilles in the Caribbean and the Atlantic, including what is now the Bahamas. Why is it called Maipure? The name Maipure was given to the family by Filippo S. Gilii in 1782, after the Maipure language of Venezuela, which he used as a basis of his comparisons. It was renamed after the culturally more important Arawak language a century later. Why is Maypure called Nu-Arawak? The Arawak language family, as constituted by L. Adam, at first by the name of Maypure, has been called by Von den Steinen ""Nu-Arawak"" from the prenominal prefix ""nu-"" for the first person. This is common to all the Arawak tribes scattered along the coasts from Suriname to Guyana. Where are Wapishana and amariba spoken? Wapishana / Matisana / Wapityan / Uapixana - spoken on the Tacutu River, Mahú River, and Surumú River, territory of Rio Branco, Brazil, and in the adjoining region in Guyana. Amariba - once spoken at the sources of the Tacutu River and Rupununi River, Guyana. (Unattested.)",Yawalapití,Maipurean
5,Maipurean,https://en-academic.com/dic.nsf/enwiki/1683998,"Southern Maipurean: A. ""Western branch"":: 42. Amuesha (a.k.a. Amoesha, Yanesha’):: 43. Chamicuro (a.k.a. Chamikuro) : B. ""Central branch"":: i. Paresí group::: 44. Peresí (a.k.a. Perecís)::: 45. Saraveca (a.k.a. Sarave) ""(†)"":: ii. Waurá group::: 46. Waurá- Meinaku (a.k.a. Waurá-Mehináku)::: 47. Yawalpití (a.k.a. Yawalapití)::: 48.",Yawalapití,Maipurean
6,Yawalapití | Encyclopedia.com,https://www.encyclopedia.com/humanities/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/yawalapiti,"Yawalapití ETHNONYMS: Agah've (in the Kalapalo and Kuikuru languages), Iaualapiti, Yawalap'h (in the Mehinaku language), Yawarave",Yawalapití,Maipurean
7,Yawalapiti - YouTube Music,https://music.youtube.com/watch?v=BJD_GZniZuY&list=OLAK5uy_nCcY7XpcjLhUeL2neq2IsGK_IeJ7tkc0U,Provided to YouTube by ONErpm Yawalapiti · Thiago Duarte · Thiago de Azevedo Duarte · Rogerio Leitum · Fabio Deiverson · Davi Sartori · Fernando Rivabem Th...,Yawalapití,Maipurean
8,Yawalapiti - song and lyrics by A.F.C.R Tribo Muirapinima ...POVO YAWALAPITI | Videos & Movies on VimeoArawakan languages - Wikipedia,https://open.spotify.com/track/7bOaOQNb8yS07sbRiPYxWX,"Listen to Yawalapiti on Spotify. A.F.C.R Tribo Muirapinima · Song · 2014. POVO YAWALAPITI a film by Vincent Moon & Priscilla Telmon, Petites Planètes produced by Fernanda Abreu, Feever Filmes The Yawalapiti from Xingu are famed for their music centered around the sacred flutes. Arawakan, also known as Maipurean (also Maipuran), is a language family spoken amongst various Indigenous peoples in South America. Branches migrated to Central America and the Greater Antilles and Lesser Antilles in the Caribbean and the Atlantic, including what is now the Bahamas. Why is it called Maipure? The name Maipure was given to the family by Filippo S. Gilii in 1782, after the Maipure language of Venezuela, which he used as a basis of his comparisons. It was renamed after the culturally more important Arawak language a century later. Why is Maypure called Nu-Arawak? The Arawak language family, as constituted by L. Adam, at first by the name of Maypure, has been called by Von den Steinen ""Nu-Arawak"" from the prenominal prefix ""nu-"" for the first person. This is common to all the Arawak tribes scattered along the coasts from Suriname to Guyana. Where are Wapishana and amariba spoken? Wapishana / Matisana / Wapityan / Uapixana - spoken on the Tacutu River, Mahú River, and Surumú River, territory of Rio Branco, Brazil, and in the adjoining region in Guyana. Amariba - once spoken at the sources of the Tacutu River and Rupununi River, Guyana. (Unattested.)",Yawalapití,Maipurean
9,POVO YAWALAPITI | Videos & Movies on VimeoArawakan languages - Wikipedia,https://vimeo.com/240305986,"POVO YAWALAPITI a film by Vincent Moon & Priscilla Telmon, Petites Planètes produced by Fernanda Abreu, Feever Filmes The Yawalapiti from Xingu are famed for their music centered around the sacred flutes. Arawakan, also known as Maipurean (also Maipuran), is a language family spoken amongst various Indigenous peoples in South America. Branches migrated to Central America and the Greater Antilles and Lesser Antilles in the Caribbean and the Atlantic, including what is now the Bahamas. Why is it called Maipure? The name Maipure was given to the family by Filippo S. Gilii in 1782, after the Maipure language of Venezuela, which he used as a basis of his comparisons. It was renamed after the culturally more important Arawak language a century later. Why is Maypure called Nu-Arawak? The Arawak language family, as constituted by L. Adam, at first by the name of Maypure, has been called by Von den Steinen ""Nu-Arawak"" from the prenominal prefix ""nu-"" for the first person. This is common to all the Arawak tribes scattered along the coasts from Suriname to Guyana. Where are Wapishana and amariba spoken? Wapishana / Matisana / Wapityan / Uapixana - spoken on the Tacutu River, Mahú River, and Surumú River, territory of Rio Branco, Brazil, and in the adjoining region in Guyana. Amariba - once spoken at the sources of the Tacutu River and Rupununi River, Guyana. (Unattested.)",Yawalapití,Maipurean
